1}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "87846dac51d25444c31204b9f6de804064097370d3684dc6c0562fb83ac14733"} {"question" "q-75035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "22"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/c6bdb6a479878c3fed94923a5db19d5e55db3431b685344d54b9a384f70a60e3.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,0)=11 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=11 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =10+11+11+12 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "87846dac51d25444c31204b9f6de804064097370d3684dc6c0562fb83ac14733"} {"question" "q-75040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "22"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "44"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "52"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "44"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-122983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-122983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/7310b1302c35084aaef5a8b2316f59c0fbd806bc9314a51fe77167988d01f343.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/ce58eb1c2ee59cf97aa77fb9e840578e7a99f094b11b2e282317aad69ae31b7a.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/cec70986dea862392342377d83adbfa534ce0f3aa87cf2161c2f88eb83fbed58.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(0,2)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-122983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "87846dac51d25444c31204b9f6de804064097370d3684dc6c0562fb83ac14733"} {"question" "q-122983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-122983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-122983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9b26ea696afda59a7181f062c7c9fab4be373035678eafa0da51bf2b22f9f4b3.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{m}_{23}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $6$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}+{m}_{13}+{m}_{23}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/70e4f9cd213e0dc4429e21cacf5c375d4def1e9ba021f05e45ee6e7820c5ff36.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8135011b8384a668d4bab28282220bb0560e1ae64c2f1d623822435349067f18.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(1,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{23} & =f(2,3)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2+3+3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "87846dac51d25444c31204b9f6de804064097370d3684dc6c0562fb83ac14733"} {"question" "q-75230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "23"] ["math" "25"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y-1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/b3c2a3891860a8ad88d2f111a3e279a65398a37020ab2f0843f8475dfd38df15.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/76cbbcf7ddff625cf035d6f7e37acbc6d278734cc87648ef6a88f8ace61f731e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=0 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =0+1+1+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "87846dac51d25444c31204b9f6de804064097370d3684dc6c0562fb83ac14733"} {"question" "q-75248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "q-75248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/state.json" "file_sha256" "368fca94e02a20cb1673c4818aafc9ae1213e762579616e654fecac3250c2b75"} {"question" "e-9476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/example-9476.json" "file_sha256" "717e30db2c9162919d9e1e9fc220e25da71e2f202a7c86e7a77ccd92d02aab2e"} {"question" "e-9476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/example-9476.json" "file_sha256" "717e30db2c9162919d9e1e9fc220e25da71e2f202a7c86e7a77ccd92d02aab2e"} {"question" "e-6242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/example-6242.json" "file_sha256" "c04d50f22c1982ff7d578adc5348696c09d4ec454531fe0e71da3124847baa8e"} {"question" "e-6242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=15 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=10 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/example-6242.json" "file_sha256" "c04d50f22c1982ff7d578adc5348696c09d4ec454531fe0e71da3124847baa8e"} {"question" "e-6241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/example-6241.json" "file_sha256" "e6bc1498f97a3fe0d41bb31227bfaa7bf1c81d90487beb122600d99fd0c1f3ff"} {"question" "e-6241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0ad490af48e1db93ab9cae7e482b5eb8956aa14f8d2972816f8852179b35c4cb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a33927647d7651ce47c9e6619bd26a29ceb5cc5dd74e379bf81ea61806fb5c95.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=2+3+4+5=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/example-6241.json" "file_sha256" "e6bc1498f97a3fe0d41bb31227bfaa7bf1c81d90487beb122600d99fd0c1f3ff"}] "usage" {"q-75037" [] "e-9476" [] "q-75035" [] "e-6242" [] "e-6241" []} "basis" 2612 "retractions" [[17592186152632 :question/problem "Let $R = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {0, 1, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$."] [17592186152631 :question/problem "Let $R = [1, 3] \\cdot [1, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x + y + 1$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 2, 3}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$."] [17592186151968 :question/problem "Consider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R$. Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/bdb990e68c9b789f02af33ceaed8ea6b.png)"] [17592186151968 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 3 \\cdot m_{11} + 3 \\cdot m_{21} + m_{12} + m_{22} \\\\\n&= 3m_{11} + 3m_{21} + m_{12} + m_{22}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151862 :question/problem "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 2, 3}, P_{2} = {0, 1, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$."] [17592186151862 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/46cc42002ea537e8af73cd4dc56915ef.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/5c7c567ad1a1236e0bed12cbb74d8657.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/2c0d05fce61626b7c70063064b2eb549.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{11} &= f(2, 1) = 15 \\\\\nm_{21} &= f(3, 1) = 10 \\\\\nm_{12} &= f(2, 2) = 12 \\\\\nm_{22} &= f(3, 2) = 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L$, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44\n$$"] [17592186151861 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$."] [17592186151861 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/64b9a595613bbab374eb084adcd919f8.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/cf15a413bc2fd256a2782ed519c67d13.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/8877f481ed010f430a9590b25e256f00.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{11} &= f(0, 1) = 2 \\\\\nm_{21} &= f(1, 1) = 3 \\\\\nm_{12} &= f(0, 2) = 4 \\\\\nm_{22} &= f(1, 2) = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L$, we get\n\n$$\nL(f, P) = 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 14\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186150697 17592186150698 17592186150699 17592186150700 17592186150701 17592186150703 17592186150704 17592186150705 17592186150706 17592186150707] "immutable_fields" [[17592186150696 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150696 :answer-field/id] [17592186150696 :answer-field/key] [17592186150696 :answer-field/type] [17592186150702 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150702 :answer-field/id] [17592186150702 :answer-field/key] [17592186150702 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
